statement.raw_tex:
\begin{theorem}[convergence for noisy data]\label{thm:conv-noisy}
Let Assumptions \ref{ass:sol}(i)-(ii) and \ref{ass:stepsize}(i) be fulfilled. If the stopping index $k(\delta)\in\mathbb{N}$ is chosen such that
\begin{equation*}
  \lim_{\delta\to0^+}k(\delta)=\infty \quad\mbox{and}\quad \lim_{\delta\to0^+}\delta^2\sum_{i=1}^{k(\delta)}\eta_i = 0,
\end{equation*}
then there exists a solution $x^*\in X$ to problem \eqref{eqn:nonlin} such that
\begin{equation*}
  \lim_{\delta\to 0^+}\E[\|x_{k(\delta)}^\delta - x^*\|^2]=0.
\end{equation*}
Further, if $\mathcal{N}(F'(x^\dag))\subset\mathcal{N}(F'(x))$, then
\begin{equation*}
  \lim_{\delta\to0^+}\E[\|x_{k(\delta)}^\delta -x^\dag\|^2]=0.
\end{equation*}
\end{theorem}

